<h1>Pivot Conveyors: Wheel, Ball Transfer &amp; Turntable</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A pivot conveyor changes product flow direction by rotating the conveying surface or using multi-directional elements that redirect products at any angle. Pivot conveyors include pivot wheel sections, <a href="/conveyor-functions/ball-transfer-tables/">ball transfer tables</a>, and turntable pivots. These systems route products between <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> lines, <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">belt conveyors</a>, and processing stations in packaging, assembly, and distribution environments where fixed-angle transfers cannot meet routing flexibility requirements.</p>
<h2 id="pivot-wheel-heading">Pivot Wheel Sections</h2>
<p>Pivot wheel sections use rows of small polyurethane or steel wheels mounted on pivoting axle assemblies. The wheels rotate freely on their axles (providing low-friction conveying), and the axle assemblies pivot to steer product direction. Pivot wheels can be set to a fixed angle for continuous directional conveying or actuated to change angle on command for programmable routing.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pivot-itoh-denki-pop-up-wheel-divert.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="View from above of a roller conveyor with rows of small wheels set between the rollers at the point where an angled roller spur branches off." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Itoh Denki&#39;s POP-D wheel divert module in a roller conveyor. Rows of small wheels sit between the rollers where an angled spur branches off the main line. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.itohdenki.com/pop-d-wheel-divert" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Itoh Denki</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Fixed-Angle Pivot Wheels</h3>
<p>Fixed-angle pivot wheels are set during installation to the required routing angle. The wheels remain at that angle permanently. Products arriving on the pivot wheel section follow the wheel angle to the discharge point. Unlike a <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/bump-turn/">bump turn</a>, which stops the product against a rail before carrying it off sideways, pivot wheels steer the product continuously with no impact.</p>
<p>Fixed pivot wheel sections suit light products like cartons, trays, and small cases. The wheels require no power when used in gravity or momentum-fed applications. Powered versions use a belt or drive mechanism beneath the wheels for positive propulsion.</p>
<h3>Actuated Pivot Wheels (Pop-Up Wheel Sorters)</h3>
<p>Actuated pivot wheels rise above and retract below a roller conveyor surface on command. When retracted, the rollers convey the product straight. When activated, the angled wheels pop up above the rollers and divert the product at the set angle. Pop-up wheel sorters are a high-rate divert mechanism used in <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>.</p>
<p>Belted versions place the wheels between narrow belts. <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/sortation/pivot-wheel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's belted pivot wheel sorter</a> puts pivoting wheels at the infeed and exit belts of each divert. The wheel angle creates a gentle divert trajectory that limits product rotation. Rate and weight limits depend on the model, so get them from the supplier. Multiple pop-up wheel sections along a trunk line each serve a dedicated sort lane.</p>
<h2 id="ball-transfer-heading">Ball Transfer Tables</h2>
<p>Ball transfer tables use a grid of omnidirectional ball bearings (ball transfer units) mounted flush in a flat table surface. Each ball unit contains a large load-bearing ball sitting on a hemispherical cup of smaller support balls. The load ball rotates freely in any direction, allowing products placed on the table to slide in any direction with minimal friction.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pivot-omni-ball-transfer-table.webp" width="1000" height="561" alt="A steel-framed table section filled with a staggered grid of steel ball transfer units." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An Omni Metalcraft ball transfer table. The grid of ball transfer units lets a load move in any direction across the table. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://omni.com/products/gravity-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Omni Metalcraft</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Passive Ball Transfer Tables</h3>
<p>Passive ball transfer tables rely on manual pushing, gravity tilt, or external conveyors to move products across the ball field. Workers push packages across the table to reorient them, rotate them, or redirect them to different conveyor spurs. Gravity-tilted ball tables use a slight incline to move products from a high side to a low side, with guide rails to control the path.</p>
<p>Table capacity depends on the rating of each ball unit and how many balls the product bottom spans at once. Size the ball spacing so the smallest product always rests on several balls, and check that the load per ball stays within the unit's rating. Heavy-duty ball units are available for pallet-size loads.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Ball Transfers</h3>
<p>Pop-up ball transfers mount between <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> rollers and rise above the roller surface when activated. The balls engage the product bottom and allow multi-directional movement. External pushers, guide rails, or downstream conveyors pull the product in the desired direction across the ball field. When deactivated, the balls retract and the rollers convey the product straight.</p>
<p>Pop-up ball transfers suit applications where products must route to one of several directions from a single decision point. They handle light loads and provide the most routing flexibility of any <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyor</a> type. However, they lack positive drive, the balls reduce friction but do not propel the product. An external force must move the product across the ball field.</p>
<h3>Powered Ball Transfer Modules</h3>
<p>Powered ball transfer modules use driven balls that rotate in a controlled direction, providing positive propulsion across the table surface. Modules use individually driven balls or zones of balls driven by a common mechanism. Powered ball transfers eliminate the need for external pushers and handle higher throughput than passive ball tables.</p>
<h2 id="turntable-heading">Turntable Pivot Systems</h2>
<p>A turntable pivot rotates the entire conveyor section (and the product on it) to any desired angle. The product enters the turntable on the onboard conveyor, the turntable rotates to align with the target discharge direction, and the onboard conveyor drives the product off. Turntables handle the heaviest loads and the widest range of rotation angles of any pivot type.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pivot-omni-powered-turntable-cdlr.webp" width="1000" height="561" alt="A round orange turntable carrying a roller conveyor deck with a yellow guard, with a drive motor mounted at the base." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An Omni Metalcraft powered turntable with a chain-driven live roller deck. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://omni.com/products/supplemental-equipment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Omni Metalcraft</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Case-Handling Turntables</h3>
<p>Case-handling turntables rotate the case and its onboard conveyor, typically in a few seconds per 90-degree turn. The onboard conveyor is typically a short <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> or belt section. A servo motor or gearmotor drives the rotation through a slewing bearing or ring gear. Position sensors and encoders provide angle feedback for precise alignment with discharge conveyors.</p>
<p>Case turntables serve applications where the product must physically rotate (barcode to a different side, label face to a scanner) and where the exit direction varies (one of several discharge paths at different angles).</p>
<h3>Pallet-Handling Turntables</h3>
<p>Pallet turntables handle loaded pallets. The onboard conveyor is a <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR (chain-driven live roller)</a> or chain conveyor section. The turntable rotates on a heavy-duty slewing bearing driven by a gearmotor. Rotation is slower than case turntables because of the mass involved, and 180 or 270-degree moves take longer than 90.</p>
<p>Pallet turntables serve two functions: direction change and pallet rotation. Direction change routes the pallet to a different conveyor line. Pallet rotation orients the pallet for stretch wrapping (spinning the pallet under a wrap head), label application on a specific face, or pallet pattern orientation for automated storage. Turntables are a standard element of <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyor systems</a>.</p>
<h3>Continuous-Rotation Turntables</h3>
<p>Some turntable applications require continuous rotation rather than indexed positioning. Stretch wrap turntables spin the pallet continuously while the stretch film carriage rises and lowers. Inspection turntables rotate the product slowly past fixed cameras or sensors for 360-degree surface inspection. These are not directional routing devices but product processing stations built on turntable platforms.</p>
