<h2 id="apps-heading">Pivot Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Manual workstation routing:</strong> Ball transfer tables at manual pack stations allow workers to receive products from one direction, rotate or reposition them for inspection or packing, and push them onto one of several discharge conveyors. The omnidirectional ball surface lets workers handle products freely without lifting. Ergonomic workstations in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a> use ball transfer tables between process steps.</p>
<p><strong>High-speed sortation diverting:</strong> Pop-up wheel sorters in <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a> divert packages from trunk lines to sort lanes. The wheels pop up, redirect the package onto the angled sort spur, and retract before the next package arrives. Pop-up wheel sorters are a core technology in e-commerce distribution centers handling parcel and small-package sorting.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet direction change and rotation:</strong> Pallet turntables in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet handling systems</a> rotate loaded pallets for stretch wrapping, reorient pallets for label application, and route pallets between multiple conveyor lines. A turntable at a conveyor intersection serves several discharge directions from a single mechanism.</p>
<p><strong>Assembly line indexing:</strong> Turntable pivots in assembly lines rotate workpieces to present different faces to sequential assembly stations. The turntable indexes 90 or 180 degrees between stations, allowing fastening, welding, or inspection on multiple product faces without manual repositioning.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-directional <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> release:</strong> Ball transfer sections at the end of accumulation lanes allow products to release in any direction. Products accumulated in queue release onto the ball section and get pushed or guided to the next available downstream conveyor. This provides flexible routing when downstream conditions change dynamically.</p>
<p><strong>Shipping and receiving staging:</strong> Ball transfer tables in shipping areas allow workers to slide packages from one position to another for manifesting, labeling, and loading. Packages arrive on a conveyor, transfer onto the ball table, get processed, and slide onto the outbound conveyor or directly into a truck.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Pivot Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Routing flexibility requirements define the type.</strong> Fixed direction changes (always 90 degrees to the same lane) use pivot wheels, <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/bump-turn/">bump turns</a>, or pop-up transfers. A <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/solutions/pop-up-transfers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">90-degree pop-up transfer</a> uses conveyors moving perpendicular to each other to change direction. Variable direction changes (different angles, different lanes each time) use ball transfers or turntables. High-speed automated diverting uses pop-up wheel sorters.</p>
<p><strong>Load weight narrows options quickly.</strong> Light cases: any pivot type works. Heavier cases: heavy-duty ball tables or case turntables. Pallets: pallet turntables with heavy-duty slewing bearings and CDLR onboard conveyors. Confirm the rated load of each specific unit.</p>
<p><strong>Throughput drives the speed requirement.</strong> Pop-up wheel sorters handle the highest throughput. Pivot wheel sections handle continuous flow. Ball transfer tables suit low-throughput manual operations. Turntables are usually the throughput bottleneck because each load must stop, rotate, and discharge, so check the cycle time against your peak rate.</p>
<p><strong>Automation level sets the control complexity.</strong> Passive ball tables and fixed pivot wheels need no controls. Pop-up wheel sorters require PLC integration, barcode scanning, and real-time routing logic. Turntables need servo or VFD position control with encoder feedback. Turntables and pop-up mechanisms create shear and pinch points, so specify guarding per <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>. Match the control complexity to your existing <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automation infrastructure</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Product orientation behavior matters.</strong> Pivot wheels partly rotate the product as they steer it. Bump turns and pop-up transfers move the product sideways without rotating it, so a different face leads. Ball tables let the operator rotate the product or keep it square. Turntables rotate the product by the table rotation angle. Select based on whether orientation change is acceptable, desired, or prohibited for downstream operations like <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/">merging and sorting</a>.</p>
